WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.170
MAX SALTONSTALL: Hi.

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:02.720
I'm Max Saltonstall,
and I'm here

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:05.820
to show you Chronicle, Google's
cloud-native security analytics

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:06.750
platform.

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:09.300
Security systems
and their operators

00:00:09.300 --> 00:00:12.090
are suffering from
intense data overload.

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.640
We see companies generating
petabytes of security data

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.920
to sift through, slowing
down the analysis

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.790
and making costs skyrocket.

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:22.500
Chronicle Security Analytics
solves these problems,

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.230
putting threat tools,
detection, intelligence,

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:30.390
and forensic signals into
one scalable tool set.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:32.310
There are six things
that set Chronicle apart

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.740
from traditional security
incident and event management

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:35.550
products.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:38.340
I'll walk you through
each one briefly.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:39.720
Beyond just
ingesting and storing

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.000
all the data you throw
at it, Chronicle also

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.370
makes it useful automatically,
so you can easily

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:48.000
map people to hosts,
to IPs, and track down

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:50.630
the exact sequence of events
leading to an incident.

00:00:53.650 --> 00:00:56.030
We get major benefits from
building on Google Search

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:56.960
components.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:00:59.960
Chronicle is continuously
re-evaluating log activity

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.700
against threat intel.

00:01:01.700 --> 00:01:03.620
When intel changes,
we automatically

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:06.650
reapply it against all
historical activity.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:09.460
No other SIEM does this.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:11.570
Five login failures
in two minutes

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.210
is no longer a
useful threat rule.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:17.660
We extended YARA, an open
standard for malware rule

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.000
writing, to handle
event logs so that you

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.830
can write the types of rules to
detect subtle malware attacks.

00:01:24.830 --> 00:01:28.520
Another perk of using Google
infrastructure is speed.

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.440
Even searches against
petabytes of data

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:32.120
take less than a second.

00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.400
And because Chronicle is
a purpose-built security

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.980
application, clients
find that tier-1 analysts

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.900
are much more productive.

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:41.420
Most organizations
spend more time

00:01:41.420 --> 00:01:43.880
managing and tuning their
SIEM than they do using it

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.950
for security operations.

00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:48.620
With Chronicle, you don't
buy servers or storage,

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.750
and you don't tune anything.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:52.730
Your team can work on
operations and analytics

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.920
and protect your business.

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.480
Get the benefit of
cloud operations

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.800
and get your analysts out of
the business of IT management.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.320
Chronicle rewrites
the economics of SIEM.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:08.389
We don't charge by the byte
or for ingest or for servers,

00:02:08.389 --> 00:02:10.970
storage, or even compute.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.370
You get a predictable
fixed price

00:02:13.370 --> 00:02:16.400
based on the size of your
organization in people.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.767
One simple price, all in.

00:02:18.767 --> 00:02:20.600
And it won't break the
bank, because pricing

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:23.330
is based on heads not bytes.

00:02:23.330 --> 00:02:25.890
We want to encourage you to
have as much data as possible

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.130
in Chronicle and never get
penalized because you've

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:31.370
gathered more logs to analyze.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.930
How do we make all
that magic happen?

00:02:33.930 --> 00:02:35.900
Let's take a look
at the architecture.

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.600
The data layer enables you
to send all your security

00:02:38.600 --> 00:02:40.310
telemetry and
threat intelligence

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.110
into a private
container, where it

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:45.350
is augmented with additional
threat intelligence.

00:02:45.350 --> 00:02:47.240
Within your private
cloud container,

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.660
your security telemetry
is normalized, enriched,

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.740
de-duplicated, and
linked together

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:56.180
into a coherent timeline for
analysis and investigation.

00:02:56.180 --> 00:02:59.750
Finally, that data is exposed
via our investigation hunting

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:02.420
and detection capabilities,
as well as to other security

00:03:02.420 --> 00:03:04.400
products, via APIs.

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.300
In addition to your
data, stored privately

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.200
in its own encrypted
instance, we

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:10.940
can bring in third-party signals
and curated internal data

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:13.250
to enhance your ability
to detect and respond

00:03:13.250 --> 00:03:15.380
to threats quickly.

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.870
There are three ways customers
are using Chronicle today.

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:20.090
Extend your current SIEM
with high-volume data

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.460
that today you throw away.

00:03:22.460 --> 00:03:25.670
For example at Google, we found
that DNS data is incredibly

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:27.530
useful for investigation
and detection,

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.170
but very few companies
actually capture it.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.810
Almost no one keeps a
year of it in their SIEM.

00:03:32.810 --> 00:03:35.480
But we keep all your security
telemetry for an entire year

00:03:35.480 --> 00:03:38.000
and make it
instantly accessible.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:40.640
Enhance your event detection
and response capabilities

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.370
by keeping the data for
more than a week or month.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:45.980
EDR generates a ton of data,
and the vendors struggle as much

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:47.630
as customers to keep it.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.090
We can keep a year of
it, make it searchable,

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:52.490
and correlate it
to other telemetry.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:54.710
Evolve your old and rusty SIEMs.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.050
They're overdue for an
update, and cloud technologies

00:03:57.050 --> 00:03:59.690
can make them faster
and more efficient.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:02.720
You can deploy Chronicle
in days rather than months

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:05.783
and transform your security
operations metrics.

00:04:05.783 --> 00:04:07.950
Check out chronicle.security
to learn more about how

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.242
we can help you extend your
current security management

00:04:10.242 --> 00:04:12.500
platform, enhance your
detection and response,

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:14.930
and evolve from
outdated systems.

00:04:14.930 --> 00:04:17.680
[MUSIC FADING OUT]

